00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:14,740
(Watch on KonFlix.vip)

1
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:39,060
WE ARE ALL TRYING HERE

2
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:52,282
Hello, everyone!

3
00:00:52,365 --> 00:00:53,449
-Hello!
-Hello!

4
00:00:58,663 --> 00:00:59,956
We've got this!

5
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:05,503
It's the end of the year!
Let's make some money!

6
00:01:11,885 --> 00:01:14,637
THE LOYAL CATERING

7
00:01:19,392 --> 00:01:20,560
All right!

8
00:01:20,643 --> 00:01:21,936
-You're good to go!
-Okay.

9
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:23,146
-Drive safely!
-Will do!

10
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:53,301
Have you ever asked for help before?

11
00:01:53,927 --> 00:01:55,678
I'm not talking about

12
00:01:56,304 --> 00:01:57,305
simply asking for help.

13
00:01:58,223 --> 00:01:59,515
I mean, when you're really struggling.

14
00:02:00,099 --> 00:02:01,392
Who'd do that?

15
00:02:01,976 --> 00:02:03,686
Make sure you ask me, okay?

16
00:02:15,281 --> 00:02:19,244
There's a worst kind of feeling
I never want to go through.

17
00:02:20,411 --> 00:02:21,287
It's hard

18
00:02:22,163 --> 00:02:23,665
to put into words.

19
00:02:24,415 --> 00:02:26,751
It's like being trapped in a hole

20
00:02:27,877 --> 00:02:32,423
that's only 30 cm wide but 100 m deep,
unable to move an inch.

21
00:02:32,507 --> 00:02:33,675
That's what it's like,

22
00:02:34,425 --> 00:02:37,095
but now that I know I can ask for help,

23
00:02:38,221 --> 00:02:40,848
that 30 cm hole

24
00:02:42,225 --> 00:02:45,895
feels about a meter wide now.

25
00:02:48,439 --> 00:02:49,524
So promise me

26
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:53,903
that you'll ask for help, okay?

27
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:55,863
Okay.

28
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:03,579
<i>To pull you out from self-destructing,</i>

29
00:03:04,706 --> 00:03:07,291
<i>I threw myself into that emotion…</i>

30
00:03:10,044 --> 00:03:13,297
<i>dove deep into it,</i>
<i>and found the words to save you.</i>

31
00:03:14,799 --> 00:03:16,718
<i>Words I've never spoken in my life.</i>

32
00:03:18,302 --> 00:03:19,220
"Help me."

33
00:03:21,389 --> 00:03:23,808
<i>But the words I found to save you</i>

34
00:03:24,934 --> 00:03:26,310
<i>ended up saving me instead.</i>

35
00:03:32,692 --> 00:03:36,946
NO ONE COMES INTO YOUR LIFE BY MISTAKE

36
00:03:43,453 --> 00:03:45,955
<i>When you get out of</i>
<i>a worst-case situation,</i>

37
00:03:46,039 --> 00:03:48,666
<i>it suddenly opens up a new perspective,</i>

38
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:52,962
<i>and everything you struggled with before</i>
<i>starts to feel trivial.</i>

39
00:03:54,088 --> 00:03:55,506
<i>Success?</i>

40
00:03:55,590 --> 00:03:56,799
<i>Debuting?</i>

41
00:03:57,425 --> 00:03:58,343
<i>Screw all that.</i>

42
00:03:59,469 --> 00:04:01,679
<i>If I had just one wish in life,</i>

43
00:04:02,889 --> 00:04:04,349
<i>it would be to die of old age.</i>

44
00:04:05,725 --> 00:04:08,227
<i>I hope cats, trees,</i>

45
00:04:08,311 --> 00:04:11,105
<i>and all living things die of old age.</i>

46
00:04:12,857 --> 00:04:14,317
<i>Not from illness,</i>

47
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:17,028
<i>not from agony.</i>

48
00:04:18,696 --> 00:04:20,490
<i>I hope everything dies of old age</i>

49
00:04:21,616 --> 00:04:23,076
<i>like leaves falling from a tree.</i>

50
00:04:30,917 --> 00:04:31,834
MA JAE-YEONG

51
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:33,920
<i>I'll credit you on the screenplay.</i>

52
00:04:34,879 --> 00:04:35,922
<i>But under a pseudonym.</i>

53
00:04:36,547 --> 00:04:38,007
<i>I refuse to put your real name on it.</i>

54
00:04:38,633 --> 00:04:39,717
<i>That's the best I can do.</i>

55
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:49,602
<i>Is it okay to use "Yeong-sil"</i>

56
00:04:49,685 --> 00:04:51,312
<i>as my pseudonym?</i>

57
00:04:52,146 --> 00:04:54,565
<i>I like the idea</i>
<i>of living an easygoing life.</i>

58
00:04:56,025 --> 00:04:57,151
<i>I'd be grateful.</i>

59
00:04:57,777 --> 00:04:59,362
<i>I'm sure Yeong-sil would be honored too.</i>

60
00:05:00,905 --> 00:05:01,823
<i>Thank you.</i>

61
00:05:12,458 --> 00:05:13,709
<i>Yeong-sil.</i>

62
00:05:17,505 --> 00:05:18,589
<i>What's that?</i>

63
00:05:19,424 --> 00:05:20,383
<i>My pseudonym.</i>

64
00:05:20,925 --> 00:05:21,926
<i>Yeong-sil.</i>

65
00:05:26,222 --> 00:05:27,974
You know what I mean.

66
00:05:28,057 --> 00:05:29,892
It's like when a guy
who has nothing going for him,

67
00:05:29,976 --> 00:05:32,687
but once a woman says he does,
he starts to seem that way.

68
00:05:32,770 --> 00:05:34,856
You start to think,
"He must have something I'm not aware of."

69
00:05:36,023 --> 00:05:38,359
Now that you like Hwang Dong-man,
he seems impressive.

70
00:05:38,943 --> 00:05:39,944
-Really?
-Yeah.

71
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,030
I'll have to tell him that.

72
00:05:45,950 --> 00:05:47,326
Wow, he's really fast.

73
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:49,036
That's amazing.

74
00:05:53,958 --> 00:05:56,169
-He's so fast.
-Wow, look at this.

75
00:05:57,336 --> 00:05:58,337
I'll leave you to it.

76
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:00,214
Wait, but…

77
00:06:02,758 --> 00:06:04,635
My brother
really went to Daegwallyeong this time.

78
00:06:16,981 --> 00:06:19,233
Eun-a, have you been waiting long?

79
00:06:19,901 --> 00:06:21,277
-I just got here.
-I see.

80
00:06:25,448 --> 00:06:26,824
TANGERINE
5,000 WON

81
00:06:26,908 --> 00:06:27,867
That looks good.

82
00:06:28,826 --> 00:06:30,870
A time of serenity is approaching,

83
00:06:30,953 --> 00:06:34,040
when you can curl up under a blanket,
eat tangerines, and read comic books.

84
00:06:34,624 --> 00:06:37,043
Why does winter feel more serene?

85
00:06:39,587 --> 00:06:41,339
Because it makes you
go looking for warmth?

86
00:06:42,048 --> 00:06:43,090
You're right.

87
00:06:44,592 --> 00:06:47,803
By the way, I think I can send you
my revised draft this week.

88
00:06:47,887 --> 00:06:50,765
The screenplay
is getting more interesting.

89
00:06:51,515 --> 00:06:52,767
I'm excited to read it.

90
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:58,272
Now I see why directors are eager
for you to tear apart their screenplays.

91
00:06:58,356 --> 00:07:00,858
When you send screenplays
to other producers,

92
00:07:00,942 --> 00:07:05,029
they just go on about the story structure
and the ending,

93
00:07:05,112 --> 00:07:07,782
throwing around technical terms.

94
00:07:07,865 --> 00:07:09,325
But you're different.

95
00:07:09,909 --> 00:07:11,535
You know exactly where I'm stuck

96
00:07:11,619 --> 00:07:15,122
and where I need to focus emotionally
so I can really take off.

97
00:07:16,457 --> 00:07:17,667
You know,

98
00:07:18,251 --> 00:07:20,378
you have a real gift for reading people.

99
00:07:21,128 --> 00:07:22,463
I guess that's a skill

100
00:07:23,297 --> 00:07:24,423
you're just born with, right?

101
00:07:26,259 --> 00:07:27,718
Come on.

102
00:07:27,802 --> 00:07:30,012
You're the one who figured out
the "help me" emotion.

103
00:07:51,409 --> 00:07:52,285
May I ask why…

104
00:07:54,662 --> 00:07:56,831
that appeared on your watch?

105
00:08:01,252 --> 00:08:02,378
You don't have to answer.

106
00:08:07,383 --> 00:08:08,634
It was because of Jin-man.

107
00:08:19,061 --> 00:08:20,021
May I ask why

108
00:08:21,063 --> 00:08:22,982
that appeared on your watch?

109
00:08:26,235 --> 00:08:28,279
You don't have to answer me.

110
00:08:33,701 --> 00:08:34,702
It was because of my mom.

111
00:08:40,041 --> 00:08:41,334
Did you get along with your mom?

112
00:08:43,085 --> 00:08:44,045
We got along better

113
00:08:45,296 --> 00:08:46,505
after she passed away.

114
00:08:50,926 --> 00:08:53,220
I don't understand the phrase
"Look after your mom."

115
00:08:54,722 --> 00:08:57,600
My idea of a mom is so different
from everyone else's.

116
00:09:00,186 --> 00:09:01,854
Whenever I feel like I'm being abandoned,

117
00:09:02,897 --> 00:09:04,815
like my boyfriend leaving me

118
00:09:06,150 --> 00:09:07,735
or things going badly at work…

119
00:09:10,196 --> 00:09:12,406
my whole body aches, and I get nosebleeds.

120
00:09:13,783 --> 00:09:16,744
When I traced these symptoms
back to the source,

121
00:09:18,412 --> 00:09:20,122
they led to one person.

122
00:09:21,957 --> 00:09:23,918
The first person who wrote me off.

123
00:09:26,337 --> 00:09:27,171
My mom.

124
00:09:31,050 --> 00:09:32,635
I didn't know when I was young,

125
00:09:34,053 --> 00:09:36,013
but when I got older, I thought,

126
00:09:38,015 --> 00:09:39,934
"The woman who gave birth to me

127
00:09:41,185 --> 00:09:42,061
<i>was such…</i>

128
00:09:44,605 --> 00:09:46,065
<i>a weak woman."</i>

129
00:09:51,195 --> 00:09:52,697
That's when I decided

130
00:09:53,531 --> 00:09:56,117
that I'm going to become a strong mother
and a strong woman.

131
00:09:57,451 --> 00:09:59,120
Someone who wouldn't be ashamed

132
00:09:59,203 --> 00:10:01,831
of her child or her husband.

133
00:10:02,456 --> 00:10:04,500
A strong mother and a strong woman.

134
00:10:07,461 --> 00:10:08,462
That's a relief.

135
00:10:09,714 --> 00:10:10,840
You see,

136
00:10:10,923 --> 00:10:13,134
I'm a really embarrassing guy.

137
00:10:14,552 --> 00:10:15,970
I wouldn't say "really."

138
00:10:18,180 --> 00:10:19,306
Just a little.

139
00:10:22,768 --> 00:10:24,145
You're a little embarrassing

140
00:10:25,020 --> 00:10:27,898
but very lovable.

141
00:11:17,156 --> 00:11:18,782
The goddess of the wind!

142
00:11:36,217 --> 00:11:38,302
<i>Who would dare abandon you?</i>

143
00:11:39,094 --> 00:11:42,640
<i>Who would dare write you off?</i>

144
00:11:43,891 --> 00:11:44,975
<i>Feel free to use me</i>

145
00:11:45,684 --> 00:11:48,354
<i>until I'm no longer needed.</i>

146
00:11:49,813 --> 00:11:51,524
<i>I'll be here waiting for you</i>
<i>if you return.</i>

147
00:11:51,607 --> 00:11:53,526
<i>The goddess of the wind!</i>

148
00:11:53,609 --> 00:11:55,486
<i>I'm a lifelong Byeon Eun-a admirer</i>

149
00:11:56,111 --> 00:11:58,113
<i>and someone you can come to anytime.</i>

150
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:00,157
<i>It's the goddess of the wind!</i>

151
00:12:00,241 --> 00:12:03,077
<i>If it pains you to see me live like that,</i>

152
00:12:03,827 --> 00:12:04,995
<i>I'll meet another woman</i>

153
00:12:05,704 --> 00:12:06,539
<i>for a little while.</i>

154
00:12:07,748 --> 00:12:09,333
<i>Just for a moment.</i>

155
00:12:10,334 --> 00:12:11,877
<i>And if you tell me to come back,</i>

156
00:12:11,961 --> 00:12:13,754
<i>I'll run back to you.</i>

157
00:12:14,463 --> 00:12:16,423
<i>No, if I meet another woman,</i>

158
00:12:17,883 --> 00:12:19,593
<i>I'll say, "Byeon Eun-a controls me."</i>

159
00:12:20,636 --> 00:12:23,430
<i>"I must rush back to her</i>
<i>whenever she calls."</i>

160
00:12:24,348 --> 00:12:26,016
<i>This is what I'll say</i>
<i>before seeing someone.</i>

161
00:12:26,934 --> 00:12:28,727
<i>If you want me to remain single</i>

162
00:12:29,937 --> 00:12:32,106
<i>or be on standby forever,</i>

163
00:12:32,189 --> 00:12:33,524
<i>I'll do that.</i>

164
00:12:34,608 --> 00:12:37,194
<i>If that's enough to ease your anxiety,</i>

165
00:12:38,612 --> 00:12:40,739
<i>isn't that a wonderful way to live?</i>

166
00:12:41,615 --> 00:12:43,909
Jeez, what am I going to do?

167
00:12:45,035 --> 00:12:46,870
I'm such a lovable bastard.

168
00:12:49,039 --> 00:12:49,999
<i>Shouldn't you head up?</i>

169
00:12:52,418 --> 00:12:53,961
Go upstairs already.

170
00:12:54,044 --> 00:12:55,296
The writer will be here soon.

171
00:12:55,379 --> 00:12:57,923
What's the point?
It's not happening anyway.

172
00:13:05,681 --> 00:13:06,765
Have you read their writing?

173
00:13:06,849 --> 00:13:08,309
I can't even look at it.

174
00:13:08,892 --> 00:13:11,145
There's a typo in the first line.
Are they insane?

175
00:13:11,228 --> 00:13:14,815
A real writer reviews their work
at least 20 times before submitting.

176
00:13:15,899 --> 00:13:17,067
Should I tell them not to come

177
00:13:17,693 --> 00:13:19,111
20 minutes before the appointment?

178
00:13:19,695 --> 00:13:21,155
Should I tell them to go back

179
00:13:21,238 --> 00:13:22,948
when they're coming
all the way from Dongducheon?

180
00:13:23,032 --> 00:13:26,035
I told you I'm not co-writing,
so why'd you set up an appointment?

181
00:13:26,118 --> 00:13:27,328
I warned you.

182
00:13:27,411 --> 00:13:28,912
You can't do it alone this time.

183
00:13:29,955 --> 00:13:31,165
Go upstairs.

184
00:13:31,248 --> 00:13:33,417
Go upstairs, interview the writer,

185
00:13:34,043 --> 00:13:36,337
and tear them apart
if that's what you want.

186
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:41,175
But I'll search the whole country
to find you a co-writer.

187
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:44,470
Just go upstairs.

188
00:13:45,429 --> 00:13:47,514
If an appointment was made,
you should show up.

189
00:13:54,188 --> 00:13:56,231
-You said to tear them apart!
-Go ahead!

190
00:13:57,441 --> 00:13:58,776
Man, that punk.

191
00:13:59,401 --> 00:14:00,861
Go easy on him.

192
00:14:01,445 --> 00:14:02,488
You know how he is.

193
00:14:03,489 --> 00:14:04,365
He's the type

194
00:14:05,199 --> 00:14:06,700
who needs constant praise

195
00:14:06,784 --> 00:14:09,912
to get his brain to kick in
and think clearly.

196
00:14:09,995 --> 00:14:10,871
What is he, a kid?

197
00:14:16,418 --> 00:14:19,421
Write something already
instead of sitting around all day.

198
00:14:20,005 --> 00:14:20,881
Hey, you…

199
00:14:24,802 --> 00:14:26,637
My brain's working.

200
00:14:27,221 --> 00:14:28,514
RESUME
NAME: PARK JEONG-MIN

201
00:14:28,597 --> 00:14:30,766
I thought you were a guy
because of your name.

202
00:14:30,849 --> 00:14:32,309
I get that a lot.

203
00:14:34,269 --> 00:14:37,147
They say female writers
hate male writers the most.

204
00:14:40,693 --> 00:14:42,653
We don't like female writers either

205
00:14:42,736 --> 00:14:45,614
because they're full of themselves
and stubborn as hell.

206
00:14:45,698 --> 00:14:48,325
That's how we see male writers too.

207
00:14:54,248 --> 00:14:56,250
-I see you've done a lot of co-writing.
-Yes.

208
00:14:56,333 --> 00:14:57,710
I haven't worked solo yet.

209
00:14:57,793 --> 00:14:58,669
Why?

210
00:14:59,837 --> 00:15:02,089
I'm not at that level yet.

211
00:15:02,172 --> 00:15:03,716
You've only been credited as a co-writer.

212
00:15:04,299 --> 00:15:06,301
-Yes.
-Did the lead writers find you helpful?

213
00:15:06,385 --> 00:15:08,637
Well, we're on good terms.

214
00:15:08,721 --> 00:15:10,305
You can be bad at your job
and still be on good terms

215
00:15:10,389 --> 00:15:12,474
if the lead writers
grit their teeth and bear it.

216
00:15:13,183 --> 00:15:14,309
I see.

217
00:15:15,853 --> 00:15:18,355
This is a bloody, intense screenplay.

218
00:15:18,439 --> 00:15:21,025
I'm not sure if a woman can handle it.

219
00:15:21,942 --> 00:15:23,027
You know what it's about?

220
00:15:23,110 --> 00:15:25,320
Yes, I heard from Producer Jung.

221
00:15:25,404 --> 00:15:28,323
The government takes out people
who stress out citizens.

222
00:15:28,407 --> 00:15:31,410
It's cathartic
and has the makings of a big hit.

223
00:15:31,493 --> 00:15:34,079
You think it'll be a big hit too, right?

224
00:15:34,163 --> 00:15:35,581
-Yes.
-You're right.

225
00:15:35,664 --> 00:15:38,459
Then tell me why I have to work with you.

226
00:15:40,419 --> 00:15:41,420
Excuse me?

227
00:15:41,503 --> 00:15:43,005
Let me be honest with you.

228
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:47,384
If you work with me,
you won't get any credit.

229
00:15:47,468 --> 00:15:50,679
I'll say I did it all myself.

230
00:15:53,432 --> 00:15:54,975
All the good scenes,

231
00:15:55,059 --> 00:15:56,018
all the crappy scenes,

232
00:15:56,101 --> 00:15:58,937
and all the insults and praise
will be mine.

233
00:15:59,021 --> 00:16:00,439
I hate sharing with others.

234
00:16:00,522 --> 00:16:03,108
Anyone asking for "just one bite" is dead.

235
00:16:03,192 --> 00:16:06,528
The ones I despise most are the bastards
who ask for "just one bite."

236
00:16:06,612 --> 00:16:08,030
They won't get a single bite.

237
00:16:09,573 --> 00:16:10,908
Why are you laughing? I'm serious.

238
00:16:11,492 --> 00:16:12,367
If you do this,

239
00:16:13,035 --> 00:16:16,497
you can't tell a single soul
that you worked on it,

240
00:16:16,580 --> 00:16:18,540
and you can't do any interviews, period.

241
00:16:18,624 --> 00:16:20,334
I'm the only one who'll do interviews.

242
00:16:20,417 --> 00:16:22,419
Not even the actors
will do more interviews than me!

243
00:16:22,503 --> 00:16:23,629
It'll be a big hit,

244
00:16:24,671 --> 00:16:25,964
so all the credit is mine.

245
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:29,384
I've taken all the criticism so far,
so why share the credit?

246
00:16:30,302 --> 00:16:34,014
I've still got four more writers
to meet after you,

247
00:16:34,098 --> 00:16:35,682
but I haven't read anything they sent.

248
00:16:35,766 --> 00:16:37,559
I don't read other people's writing.

249
00:16:38,227 --> 00:16:40,187
Why? Because it's boring.

250
00:16:40,270 --> 00:16:42,356
My writing is the most entertaining
in the world.

251
00:16:42,439 --> 00:16:44,608
Either way, I'm going to take full credit,

252
00:16:44,691 --> 00:16:46,777
and if I don't like your writing,
I'll fire you.

253
00:16:46,860 --> 00:16:50,405
It's fine as long as you don't go around
saying you wrote it.

254
00:16:50,489 --> 00:16:53,617
But you still have to work freaking hard.

255
00:16:54,576 --> 00:16:55,619
You still want the job?

256
00:16:58,497 --> 00:16:59,832
Why do you keep laughing? I'm serious.

257
00:16:59,915 --> 00:17:02,084
Are you confident
you can keep your mouth shut

258
00:17:02,167 --> 00:17:05,170
whenever this screenplay
comes up in conversation?

259
00:17:05,254 --> 00:17:09,174
The second you say you wrote this,
I'll go ballistic and curse you out.

260
00:17:09,925 --> 00:17:10,884
Are you up for it?

261
00:17:13,095 --> 00:17:14,138
Director Park.

262
00:17:16,348 --> 00:17:18,183
You're hilarious.

263
00:17:19,643 --> 00:17:21,979
You're the funniest director
I've ever met.

264
00:17:51,466 --> 00:17:52,467
I didn't order that.

265
00:17:55,095 --> 00:17:56,722
Why are you standing there frozen?

266
00:17:56,805 --> 00:17:57,639
Unfreeze!

267
00:18:08,150 --> 00:18:11,862
<i>Director Park, you're hilarious.</i>

268
00:18:11,945 --> 00:18:14,489
You're the funniest director
I've ever met.

269
00:18:22,122 --> 00:18:23,123
Oh jeez.

270
00:18:26,752 --> 00:18:29,421
Please knock before you come out.

271
00:18:33,091 --> 00:18:34,551
What were you looking at?

272
00:18:34,635 --> 00:18:35,636
Nothing, ma'am.

273
00:18:35,719 --> 00:18:36,553
It's a joke.

274
00:18:36,637 --> 00:18:38,180
Just kidding.

275
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:44,978
Tomorrow's interview is at 4 p.m.

276
00:18:49,399 --> 00:18:50,525
I said it's at 4 p.m.

277
00:18:51,151 --> 00:18:52,861
Why bother?
I'll go with the one I saw today.

278
00:18:56,323 --> 00:18:57,783
We just have to work together, no?

279
00:18:57,866 --> 00:18:59,993
But it should be a writer
who can actually help.

280
00:19:00,077 --> 00:19:01,245
She did help.

281
00:19:02,412 --> 00:19:03,330
Look, it doesn't matter.

282
00:19:03,413 --> 00:19:05,540
I'm writing it alone anyway,
so let's just go with her.

283
00:19:05,624 --> 00:19:07,292
You said the first line had a typo.

284
00:19:08,168 --> 00:19:10,337
-What kind of writer does that?
-Are we writing a book?

285
00:19:10,963 --> 00:19:12,297
Are we publishing it?

286
00:19:12,965 --> 00:19:14,508
Who cares about typos?

287
00:19:16,969 --> 00:19:18,095
We just need ideas.

288
00:19:18,679 --> 00:19:19,554
Does she have ideas?

289
00:19:20,222 --> 00:19:21,223
I'm sure she does.

290
00:19:28,563 --> 00:19:29,898
I said I'll work with her.

291
00:19:31,858 --> 00:19:33,151
Isn't that enough?

292
00:19:35,362 --> 00:19:38,073
<i>You're the funniest director</i>
<i>I've ever met.</i>

293
00:19:50,544 --> 00:19:52,296
<i>-Hey, what's up?</i>
-Where are you?

294
00:19:52,879 --> 00:19:54,423
I came out for a bit. Why?

295
00:19:56,216 --> 00:19:57,801
I wanted to ask you what Eun-a's up to.

296
00:19:57,884 --> 00:19:59,428
What for?

297
00:19:59,511 --> 00:20:01,596
I sent her my revised draft about…

298
00:20:03,056 --> 00:20:04,433
nine and a half hours ago.

299
00:20:05,183 --> 00:20:06,018
But there's no word.

300
00:20:06,893 --> 00:20:07,728
Are you scared?

301
00:20:08,854 --> 00:20:11,940
I pulled an all-nighter,
so I should be sleeping, but I can't.

302
00:20:13,275 --> 00:20:15,485
I sent it, but looking at it again,
it's not great.

303
00:20:16,486 --> 00:20:20,907
Hey, if Mr. Choi catches her reading it
during work hours, she'll get chewed out.

304
00:20:20,991 --> 00:20:23,035
She'll probably get back to you tonight,
so just stay scared until then.

305
00:20:23,118 --> 00:20:24,661
Oh jeez.

306
00:20:25,245 --> 00:20:28,498
Please, please, please be a good read.

307
00:20:28,582 --> 00:20:30,667
Please be a good read.

308
00:20:30,751 --> 00:20:32,461
This is Writer Park Jeong-min.

309
00:20:32,544 --> 00:20:33,628
She's a woman.

310
00:20:33,712 --> 00:20:35,547
This is Team Leader Lee In-yeong
from the streaming company.

311
00:20:35,630 --> 00:20:38,133
-Right.
-Hello, nice to meet you.

312
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:40,719
Thank you.

313
00:20:42,721 --> 00:20:45,307
I'm so relieved
you found a writer so quickly.

314
00:20:45,390 --> 00:20:49,186
We worried it might've been impolite
to suggest a co-writer to you.

315
00:20:49,269 --> 00:20:50,937
Oh, it wasn't impolite.

316
00:20:51,021 --> 00:20:52,064
It was rude.

317
00:20:56,777 --> 00:20:58,945
We just want to produce good work quickly.

318
00:20:59,738 --> 00:21:01,823
Did you hear that?
We have to write quickly.

319
00:21:02,491 --> 00:21:05,452
Jeez, we're not a factory
that can churn out writing quickly.

320
00:21:07,537 --> 00:21:09,664
I hear this is your first time co-writing.

321
00:21:09,748 --> 00:21:11,291
I hope you two work well together.

322
00:21:11,375 --> 00:21:13,210
I think co-writing is great.

323
00:21:13,293 --> 00:21:15,128
I'm going to keep doing it from now on.

324
00:21:15,712 --> 00:21:19,174
I spend more time playing games
than I do writing.

325
00:21:19,758 --> 00:21:21,093
But I have to be careful.

326
00:21:21,176 --> 00:21:22,844
"Should I play a round or not?"

327
00:21:22,928 --> 00:21:26,390
There's pressure to always look
fully absorbed in your work.

328
00:21:26,973 --> 00:21:28,683
You didn't play a single round today.

329
00:21:28,767 --> 00:21:29,810
But I did.

330
00:21:29,893 --> 00:21:32,020
I played a round in the bathroom.

331
00:21:35,273 --> 00:21:36,942
Hey, I'm heading out first.

332
00:21:37,526 --> 00:21:38,360
See you tomorrow.

333
00:21:39,611 --> 00:21:40,654
-We're leaving now.
-Goodbye.

334
00:22:00,215 --> 00:22:02,926
WEATHER MAKER
BY HWANG DONG-MAN

335
00:22:06,096 --> 00:22:07,389
I'll read it carefully.

336
00:22:20,527 --> 00:22:22,988
Let's not do this in our next life.

337
00:22:23,071 --> 00:22:24,614
It isn't worth doing.

338
00:22:25,699 --> 00:22:27,075
After working your ass off writing,

339
00:22:27,993 --> 00:22:30,912
you're on pins and needles,
like a kid waiting to be punished.

340
00:22:30,996 --> 00:22:33,498
Back in the day, it wasn't so agonizing

341
00:22:33,582 --> 00:22:35,041
for people in the arts.

342
00:22:35,125 --> 00:22:37,460
They just lived the way they were born.

343
00:22:37,544 --> 00:22:38,753
Geniuses?

344
00:22:38,837 --> 00:22:40,839
They're not geniuses for being impressive.

345
00:22:40,922 --> 00:22:42,090
They were just born that way.

346
00:22:42,716 --> 00:22:46,845
It wasn't about
someone being gifted at this or that.

347
00:22:46,928 --> 00:22:50,307
Everyone just lived naturally,
the way they were born.

348
00:22:51,057 --> 00:22:54,227
I've got no talent, but some strange urge
led me to this line of work,

349
00:22:54,311 --> 00:22:56,104
and now I'm going through hell.

350
00:22:56,688 --> 00:22:58,023
We should leave art

351
00:22:58,106 --> 00:23:00,150
for the geniuses to do.

352
00:23:01,151 --> 00:23:02,736
And leave studying for smart people.

353
00:23:04,070 --> 00:23:06,198
I'd be writing my ass off
instead of sitting around.

354
00:23:06,281 --> 00:23:09,618
Hey, what am I supposed to write
when I've got nothing?

355
00:23:09,701 --> 00:23:11,286
Should I string together the alphabet?

356
00:23:14,331 --> 00:23:16,708
Hey, why don't you give us
something like this too?

357
00:23:16,791 --> 00:23:17,918
Instead of this crap!

358
00:23:19,169 --> 00:23:21,296
Look at you, bringing out the fancy stuff
for your special guest.

359
00:23:26,218 --> 00:23:27,552
Who's here?

360
00:23:27,636 --> 00:23:28,637
Oh Jeong-hui.

361
00:23:28,720 --> 00:23:29,846
What? Oh Jeong-hui?

362
00:23:44,069 --> 00:23:45,904
She was probably terrified

363
00:23:45,987 --> 00:23:47,656
that her biological daughter
might turn up.

364
00:23:48,240 --> 00:23:49,783
Rumor has it she doesn't see her at all.

365
00:23:50,784 --> 00:23:54,663
We don't know
whether she doesn't or she can't.

366
00:23:55,956 --> 00:23:57,499
Have you worked with Oh Jeong-hui?

367
00:23:58,500 --> 00:23:59,793
-I have.
-Right.

368
00:23:59,876 --> 00:24:02,712
-One month?
-One month is enough to read someone.

369
00:24:02,796 --> 00:24:04,547
Even one day is enough.

370
00:24:04,631 --> 00:24:07,092
When I was a PA on the directing team
for <i>Dogs and Cats</i>,

371
00:24:07,175 --> 00:24:09,427
we used to drink vending machine coffee.

372
00:24:09,511 --> 00:24:12,389
Whenever I heard coins jingle,
I'd stand up right away.

373
00:24:13,014 --> 00:24:15,392
Why? Because getting coffee
was the PA's job.

374
00:24:15,475 --> 00:24:19,020
The second I heard coins jingle,
I'd jump up like Pavlov's dog.

375
00:24:19,104 --> 00:24:20,438
That day,

376
00:24:20,522 --> 00:24:23,566
I heard coins jingle and jumped up.

377
00:24:24,317 --> 00:24:25,652
Then I saw Oh Jeong-hui

378
00:24:27,070 --> 00:24:29,948
counting out coins in her hand

379
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:32,742
as she walked out.

380
00:24:34,286 --> 00:24:36,830
She actually went out
and got her own coffee.

381
00:24:36,913 --> 00:24:37,831
I fell for her right then.

382
00:24:40,083 --> 00:24:42,252
That's the sort of thing
a PA on set falls for.

383
00:24:42,961 --> 00:24:44,296
I thought,

384
00:24:44,379 --> 00:24:47,841
"When I get older,
I'll take care of my own needs too."

385
00:24:48,508 --> 00:24:50,635
"I'll never have someone else to do it."

386
00:24:51,344 --> 00:24:52,804
You know why she does that?

387
00:24:55,015 --> 00:24:55,849
She enjoys it.

388
00:24:55,932 --> 00:24:57,642
The attention, that is.

389
00:24:57,726 --> 00:24:59,519
"Hey, it's Oh Jeong-hui!"

390
00:24:59,602 --> 00:25:01,938
That's why she goes everywhere herself.

391
00:25:02,022 --> 00:25:04,524
Pharmacy, grocery store, you name it.

392
00:25:04,607 --> 00:25:08,028
That's just your cynical way
of seeing things.

393
00:25:09,321 --> 00:25:12,657
If I hadn't quit like that back then…

394
00:25:15,076 --> 00:25:18,538
I'd at least be able to
exchange greetings with her by now.

395
00:25:25,170 --> 00:25:26,421
Hello.

396
00:25:26,504 --> 00:25:27,839
Oh, that's okay.

397
00:25:44,731 --> 00:25:46,149
WEATHER MAKER
BY HWANG DONG-MAN

398
00:26:15,512 --> 00:26:16,596
Hello.

399
00:26:17,514 --> 00:26:19,307
I emailed it to you already.

400
00:26:19,391 --> 00:26:20,517
You didn't receive it?

401
00:26:22,477 --> 00:26:24,312
I'm actually in a meeting right now.

402
00:26:25,397 --> 00:26:27,732
I'll have that ready
by the end of the year.

403
00:26:43,289 --> 00:26:46,251
They said the fifth revision
will be ready for you to review

404
00:26:46,334 --> 00:26:49,129
by the end of next month.

405
00:26:54,175 --> 00:26:55,260
Hello.

406
00:26:55,343 --> 00:26:57,470
I'm not sure if you remember me,

407
00:26:57,554 --> 00:27:00,557
but my name is Hwang Dong-man,
and I worked on the directing team

408
00:27:01,266 --> 00:27:02,684
for <i>Dogs and Cats</i>.

409
00:27:03,184 --> 00:27:04,185
Is that so?

410
00:27:04,769 --> 00:27:06,688
I'm sorry,
but I don't remember you at all.

411
00:27:06,771 --> 00:27:07,605
Right.

412
00:27:08,148 --> 00:27:09,190
I'm not surprised.

413
00:27:29,252 --> 00:27:30,920
The bastard went inside the room!

414
00:27:31,004 --> 00:27:32,213
What room?

415
00:27:32,297 --> 00:27:33,798
The one Oh Jeong-hui is in!

416
00:27:35,884 --> 00:27:37,552
Go drag him out of there right now!

417
00:27:38,511 --> 00:27:39,387
Hurry!

418
00:27:41,806 --> 00:27:43,892
-Oh, whatever. That crazy bastard.
-Hey!

419
00:27:49,022 --> 00:27:52,066
You probably don't remember me
because I quit after a month,

420
00:27:52,150 --> 00:27:54,444
but what I regretted most about quitting

421
00:27:54,527 --> 00:27:57,071
was not getting to watch your acting.

422
00:27:58,948 --> 00:28:00,783
The way you could
instantly raise the intensity,

423
00:28:00,867 --> 00:28:03,953
throw yourself into the role,
and slip into character.

424
00:28:04,037 --> 00:28:07,957
I watched from the set,
holding my breath and sweating.

425
00:28:08,875 --> 00:28:13,254
I couldn't even breathe
because I thought it might distract you.

426
00:28:13,755 --> 00:28:15,548
As soon as the director said, "Cut!"

427
00:28:15,632 --> 00:28:17,634
and you finally let out your breath,

428
00:28:17,717 --> 00:28:19,636
I found myself doing the same.

429
00:28:19,719 --> 00:28:24,307
I had a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity
to watch a legendary actor perform,

430
00:28:25,183 --> 00:28:26,935
and it led to a moment of enlightenment.

431
00:28:27,602 --> 00:28:32,440
I had only understood
film and acting in theory,

432
00:28:32,524 --> 00:28:36,236
but you made it clear what they truly are,
right in front of my eyes.

433
00:28:36,861 --> 00:28:37,904
And that feeling

434
00:28:39,489 --> 00:28:40,740
has stayed with me ever since.

435
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:43,159
Thank you.

436
00:28:43,243 --> 00:28:45,286
You once looked at a screenplay and said,

437
00:28:46,704 --> 00:28:48,456
"The writing doesn't close in on me."

438
00:28:48,540 --> 00:28:49,374
"Close in."

439
00:28:50,500 --> 00:28:53,795
Hearing you say a phrase I've only heard
in the context of close-quarters combat

440
00:28:53,878 --> 00:28:55,129
really opened my eyes.

441
00:28:55,213 --> 00:28:59,717
"Close in" could only come from someone
who acts with their whole body.

442
00:29:00,343 --> 00:29:01,261
Even now,

443
00:29:02,554 --> 00:29:05,473
I ask myself,
"Is my writing closing in on me?"

444
00:29:05,557 --> 00:29:08,059
"Or am I just sitting here
overthinking it?"

445
00:29:09,102 --> 00:29:10,812
That became
a major guiding principle for me,

446
00:29:11,980 --> 00:29:13,606
and I always measure myself against it.

447
00:29:14,190 --> 00:29:16,067
I haven't heard that in a while.

448
00:29:16,150 --> 00:29:17,068
"Close in."

449
00:29:18,403 --> 00:29:20,071
I said it often back in the day.

450
00:29:20,154 --> 00:29:20,989
Yes.

451
00:29:22,407 --> 00:29:24,158
Though it was just a month,

452
00:29:24,242 --> 00:29:27,328
I learned so much
from simply observing you from a distance.

453
00:29:27,912 --> 00:29:29,998
So I really wanted to tell you

454
00:29:30,081 --> 00:29:33,209
that someone you don't even remember

455
00:29:33,793 --> 00:29:35,753
has been living by the words you once said

456
00:29:35,837 --> 00:29:39,215
as if they were
a guiding principle in his life.

457
00:29:40,633 --> 00:29:43,136
I really wanted to say that.

458
00:29:44,596 --> 00:29:45,513
Thank you.

459
00:29:46,723 --> 00:29:47,640
Thank you.

460
00:29:48,391 --> 00:29:49,684
What films have you done?

461
00:29:50,393 --> 00:29:51,603
I suddenly want to watch them.

462
00:29:59,027 --> 00:30:00,653
I haven't…

463
00:30:02,447 --> 00:30:04,490
I haven't been able to debut yet.

464
00:30:15,627 --> 00:30:17,837
Well, I'm sure you will soon.

465
00:30:19,464 --> 00:30:22,008
By the way, why did you quit back then?

466
00:30:23,092 --> 00:30:24,761
Did you get a better job offer?

467
00:30:29,974 --> 00:30:31,935
Do you remember…

468
00:30:33,895 --> 00:30:36,105
when we stopped filming for a few days…

469
00:30:39,567 --> 00:30:40,526
to remove an odor?

470
00:30:43,738 --> 00:30:45,490
Yes, that's right.

471
00:30:46,074 --> 00:30:48,993
I really had to relieve myself,

472
00:30:49,869 --> 00:30:52,372
so I had no choice
but to use the bathroom on set.

473
00:30:52,455 --> 00:30:54,749
Unfortunately, the toilet in the bathroom

474
00:30:54,832 --> 00:30:57,418
was merely a prop
placed directly on the floor.

475
00:30:58,002 --> 00:30:59,128
I had no idea

476
00:30:59,921 --> 00:31:01,047
because I was a newbie.

477
00:31:01,631 --> 00:31:03,716
I considered picking it up
and running with it,

478
00:31:03,800 --> 00:31:05,426
but it was open at the bottom,

479
00:31:06,469 --> 00:31:07,679
so I couldn't.

480
00:31:08,388 --> 00:31:12,517
I was in my twenties and very shy,
so I had no choice but to run away.

481
00:31:13,101 --> 00:31:16,229
Despite knowing the cliché
that the missing person is the culprit,

482
00:31:16,312 --> 00:31:18,189
I had no choice but to run.

483
00:31:19,023 --> 00:31:20,942
I sometimes think my debut

484
00:31:22,318 --> 00:31:23,528
would've come much sooner

485
00:31:24,779 --> 00:31:27,532
if I hadn't made that mistake.

486
00:31:32,787 --> 00:31:34,497
As your biggest fan,

487
00:31:35,123 --> 00:31:38,001
I'm putting aside my embarrassment

488
00:31:38,084 --> 00:31:39,085
to make this confession

489
00:31:39,877 --> 00:31:42,338
and to make a fresh start.

490
00:31:44,173 --> 00:31:45,008
Also,

491
00:31:45,633 --> 00:31:47,301
I sincerely congratulate you

492
00:31:48,011 --> 00:31:49,178
on your comeback

493
00:31:50,013 --> 00:31:50,847
after your setback.

494
00:32:03,943 --> 00:32:04,777
Now,

495
00:32:05,570 --> 00:32:07,530
if you'll excuse me.

496
00:32:20,168 --> 00:32:22,045
What the heck? Why are you here?

497
00:32:22,128 --> 00:32:23,379
I thought you were banned.

498
00:32:24,213 --> 00:32:25,715
The ban's been lifted.

499
00:32:32,805 --> 00:32:33,639
Lifted?

500
00:32:35,683 --> 00:32:36,517
What did he say?

501
00:32:37,101 --> 00:32:38,561
Just that he's a fan.

502
00:32:40,271 --> 00:32:41,939
That crazy bastard.

503
00:32:42,023 --> 00:32:44,442
There are always guys like him, you know.

504
00:32:44,525 --> 00:32:48,571
People who stay on the sidelines
but act as if they belong.

505
00:32:48,654 --> 00:32:52,241
He still hasn't debuted after 20 years
and is just hanging around.

506
00:32:52,992 --> 00:32:55,369
Darn, it was nice not seeing him around.

507
00:33:33,199 --> 00:33:34,283
Welcome.

508
00:33:41,249 --> 00:33:42,083
Let's go.

509
00:34:06,816 --> 00:34:09,527
<i>Don't let anyone find out</i>
<i>that the poor kid Oh Jeong-hui abandoned</i>

510
00:34:10,611 --> 00:34:12,155
<i>is actually me.</i>

511
00:34:13,698 --> 00:34:15,241
<i>I won't let anyone find out either.</i>

512
00:34:54,113 --> 00:34:55,031
Here.

513
00:34:55,907 --> 00:34:58,117
I'd like to ask for your help
with casting.

514
00:35:01,204 --> 00:35:02,288
Casting?

515
00:35:02,371 --> 00:35:03,206
Yes.

516
00:35:05,875 --> 00:35:07,668
"<i>Knock knock knock</i>"?

517
00:35:07,752 --> 00:35:09,420
What kind of title is this?

518
00:35:10,254 --> 00:35:12,256
The title must grab the actor's attention.

519
00:35:12,924 --> 00:35:14,592
The screenplay is good.

520
00:35:14,675 --> 00:35:15,968
It's well put together.

521
00:35:16,886 --> 00:35:20,014
It's by Director Ma Jae-yeong,
who won the production funding grant.

522
00:35:20,097 --> 00:35:22,808
His top choice is actor Noh Kang-sik.

523
00:35:23,893 --> 00:35:25,853
He's greedy for a newbie, isn't he?

524
00:35:25,937 --> 00:35:27,480
We're doing this on a government grant,

525
00:35:27,563 --> 00:35:30,066
so we can't work
with a big company like Choi Film,

526
00:35:30,149 --> 00:35:32,818
but if you help us,
I promise I'll make it up to you.

527
00:35:33,653 --> 00:35:35,404
I'm counting on you to put in a good word.

528
00:35:35,488 --> 00:35:37,031
Move on from projects like this.

529
00:35:37,114 --> 00:35:39,533
How long are you going to keep doing
government-funded projects?

530
00:35:40,117 --> 00:35:41,452
You have 20 years of experience.

531
00:35:41,535 --> 00:35:43,579
Do you really want to
live off government money?

532
00:35:44,163 --> 00:35:45,539
Yes, well,

533
00:35:45,623 --> 00:35:47,124
I have to make a living somehow.

534
00:35:47,208 --> 00:35:49,585
That's why you need to do
what makes money,

535
00:35:50,169 --> 00:35:51,921
not what's good.

536
00:35:52,004 --> 00:35:54,924
They say amateurs go for what's good,

537
00:35:55,007 --> 00:35:57,927
and pros go for what makes money.

538
00:35:58,010 --> 00:36:00,179
Isn't it time for you to turn pro, Ms. Ko?

539
00:36:00,263 --> 00:36:01,889
Come on, Pro Ko.

540
00:36:03,975 --> 00:36:07,687
Filmmakers act like starving
is some kind of badge of honor.

541
00:36:08,854 --> 00:36:10,356
I'll pass this on,

542
00:36:10,439 --> 00:36:11,732
but this is the last time.

543
00:36:12,316 --> 00:36:14,068
Do what makes money, all right?

544
00:36:18,489 --> 00:36:20,992
Why is Hwang Dong-man coming here again?

545
00:36:21,075 --> 00:36:22,410
He's come to his senses.

546
00:36:23,369 --> 00:36:24,704
I'll believe it when pigs fly.

547
00:36:26,247 --> 00:36:28,582
Nothing you do meets my approval.

548
00:36:32,044 --> 00:36:34,088
-You're leaving?
-I'm heading out.

549
00:36:34,171 --> 00:36:35,840
-Goodbye, sir.
-See you.

550
00:36:36,716 --> 00:36:37,550
See you.

551
00:36:43,014 --> 00:36:44,390
Jeez, it's pouring.

552
00:36:45,016 --> 00:36:45,933
Take it with you.

553
00:36:46,934 --> 00:36:48,185
Okay, thanks.

554
00:36:53,607 --> 00:36:54,608
Get home safely.

555
00:37:09,957 --> 00:37:10,958
You know…

556
00:37:13,419 --> 00:37:17,006
I can tolerate a guy sleeping around,
but not acting superior.

557
00:37:18,466 --> 00:37:20,718
And this guy keeps acting superior.

558
00:37:21,302 --> 00:37:22,345
Who do you mean?

559
00:37:24,305 --> 00:37:25,348
I'm heading home.

560
00:37:26,057 --> 00:37:27,350
-Take it easy.
-Yes, ma'am.

561
00:37:27,433 --> 00:37:28,726
I'm off.

562
00:37:28,809 --> 00:37:29,643
-See you.
-Okay.

563
00:37:49,413 --> 00:37:52,708
Is it that serious?

564
00:37:55,628 --> 00:37:58,089
It's okay. Please speak freely.

565
00:37:58,756 --> 00:38:00,257
I'll accept anything you say.

566
00:38:03,219 --> 00:38:04,970
I'll brace myself and make sure

567
00:38:05,054 --> 00:38:08,140
my face doesn't go blank
while listening to you.

568
00:38:08,891 --> 00:38:10,017
Ready.

569
00:38:10,101 --> 00:38:10,935
Action!

570
00:38:13,896 --> 00:38:15,606
Why are you so drunk?

571
00:38:16,399 --> 00:38:17,358
Because I'm scared.

572
00:38:19,068 --> 00:38:21,153
Why? It was great.

573
00:38:27,493 --> 00:38:28,619
I cried

574
00:38:29,245 --> 00:38:30,287
while reading it.

575
00:38:33,791 --> 00:38:35,418
I came running to give you a hug.

576
00:38:38,421 --> 00:38:39,713
So why are you scared?

577
00:38:40,965 --> 00:38:42,216
Oh gosh, I thought…

578
00:38:43,592 --> 00:38:45,928
Are you serious?

579
00:38:46,011 --> 00:38:48,973
The day's over, so let's drink!

580
00:38:49,932 --> 00:38:53,227
Make way! Jang Mi-ran is coming through!

581
00:38:53,310 --> 00:38:55,104
-So where are we…
-What?

582
00:38:55,980 --> 00:38:57,314
Hwang Dong-man!

583
00:38:57,398 --> 00:38:58,899
-Director Hwang!
-Mi-ran!

584
00:39:00,234 --> 00:39:01,652
-How have you been?
-I've been great.

585
00:39:02,445 --> 00:39:04,029
-Well, I…
-Enjoy your time.

586
00:39:04,113 --> 00:39:05,573
-Shall we…
-Oh my.

587
00:39:05,656 --> 00:39:07,032
-Hello, Mi-ran.
-Hello, Director Lee.

588
00:39:07,116 --> 00:39:08,284
-Gosh, you don't have to.
-Sorry?

589
00:39:11,412 --> 00:39:13,873
-Mi-ran!
-Oh, you too, Director Park?

590
00:39:18,419 --> 00:39:21,088
Why? What is it, Director Park?

591
00:39:21,172 --> 00:39:25,050
-My dear director!
-My dear actor!

592
00:39:25,134 --> 00:39:26,677
All right, let's drink!

593
00:39:26,760 --> 00:39:28,345
-Let's drink!
-Let's drink!

594
00:39:31,348 --> 00:39:33,100
Here comes the wave!

595
00:39:39,940 --> 00:39:40,858
Hey!

596
00:39:41,650 --> 00:39:43,068
Fill your glass! One more time!

597
00:39:43,152 --> 00:39:46,197
-Sounds good!
-Come on, one more shot!

598
00:39:46,780 --> 00:39:48,574
-Let's go!
-The wave started!

599
00:39:48,657 --> 00:39:50,701
Here it comes!

600
00:39:51,285 --> 00:39:53,370
Hey, that's not fair!

601
00:39:53,454 --> 00:39:54,914
Mi-ran, why aren't you drinking?

602
00:39:54,997 --> 00:39:56,957
You have to drink!

603
00:39:58,167 --> 00:39:59,460
Why aren't you drinking?

604
00:39:59,543 --> 00:40:01,545
If a scene looks well-written to me too,

605
00:40:01,629 --> 00:40:04,632
I go really hard on everyone
and do multiple takes.

606
00:40:05,674 --> 00:40:07,760
But if a scene looks bad to me,

607
00:40:07,843 --> 00:40:08,677
I shoot it quickly.

608
00:40:09,386 --> 00:40:10,804
I just rush through it

609
00:40:10,888 --> 00:40:12,598
before anyone catches on
that it's not good.

610
00:40:12,681 --> 00:40:13,849
Ready.

611
00:40:14,767 --> 00:40:16,060
Action, cut, okay!

612
00:40:16,143 --> 00:40:18,521
Cut, okay! Cut, okay!

613
00:40:18,604 --> 00:40:20,606
Cut, okay! Cut, okay! Cut, okay!

614
00:40:20,689 --> 00:40:21,941
My goodness.

615
00:40:22,024 --> 00:40:23,275
By the way,

616
00:40:23,359 --> 00:40:25,277
did you know

617
00:40:25,361 --> 00:40:26,904
there's a Dong-man in every class?

618
00:40:26,987 --> 00:40:28,614
The Dong-man
of the freshman class of 2008,

619
00:40:28,697 --> 00:40:29,990
the Dong-man
of the freshman class of 2009.

620
00:40:31,116 --> 00:40:35,829
They call the most problematic student
in that class year "Dong-man,"

621
00:40:35,913 --> 00:40:38,958
and there's always one every single year.

622
00:40:40,793 --> 00:40:43,337
There are Dong-mans in kindergarten too.

623
00:40:43,420 --> 00:40:47,091
They argue about who's closer to whom.

624
00:40:47,174 --> 00:40:51,887
They're all fighting hard
against their worthlessness too.

625
00:40:52,388 --> 00:40:54,682
-No regrets!
-What did I know?

626
00:40:54,765 --> 00:40:56,058
So I went searching for it.

627
00:40:56,141 --> 00:40:59,895
I searched and searched,
and even checked an English style guide.

628
00:41:00,479 --> 00:41:03,857
I'll never forget the first sentence
on the first page.

629
00:41:04,733 --> 00:41:08,279
"If you're not writing about sex,

630
00:41:09,738 --> 00:41:10,906
it'd better be really good."

631
00:41:13,117 --> 00:41:14,994
-Come on, how do you beat sex?
-How…

632
00:41:15,077 --> 00:41:18,205
Exactly. How do you beat that?

633
00:41:18,289 --> 00:41:19,123
You can't.

634
00:41:20,374 --> 00:41:21,584
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

635
00:41:23,794 --> 00:41:28,132
I wonder what it feels like
to call Ms. Oh Jeong-hui your mom.

636
00:41:33,220 --> 00:41:34,555
Shit?

637
00:41:34,638 --> 00:41:35,848
Shit.

638
00:41:35,931 --> 00:41:37,933
She thinks she'll go down in history.

639
00:41:38,017 --> 00:41:39,768
Wouldn't she, though?

640
00:41:39,852 --> 00:41:41,186
No, she won't.

641
00:41:41,270 --> 00:41:43,606
Everyone thinks
going down in history is easy,

642
00:41:43,689 --> 00:41:46,066
but no one ever does,
no matter how hard they try.

643
00:41:55,784 --> 00:41:58,912
Wherever I go,
I need to stand out the most.

644
00:42:00,456 --> 00:42:02,041
But you get on my nerves.

645
00:42:02,124 --> 00:42:03,500
You know you're pretty, right?

646
00:42:04,209 --> 00:42:05,586
So why do you dress carelessly?

647
00:42:06,170 --> 00:42:07,254
Are you dressed properly?

648
00:42:08,005 --> 00:42:08,881
No, you're not.

649
00:42:13,719 --> 00:42:15,429
When I was in elementary school,

650
00:42:16,013 --> 00:42:18,390
school life grew more difficult,

651
00:42:18,474 --> 00:42:20,976
so I deliberately
left my pencil case at home.

652
00:42:21,727 --> 00:42:22,895
And every day,

653
00:42:23,646 --> 00:42:26,106
I asked if I could borrow a pencil.

654
00:42:27,107 --> 00:42:29,777
I didn't know why I did that at the time,

655
00:42:31,528 --> 00:42:33,197
but I found out when I got older.

656
00:42:34,657 --> 00:42:36,700
I was trying to reduce my problem

657
00:42:37,493 --> 00:42:39,995
to just being the annoying kid
who borrowed pencils…

658
00:42:42,206 --> 00:42:44,249
because I was afraid
they'd find out my real problem.

659
00:42:47,753 --> 00:42:49,004
So that's why

660
00:42:50,172 --> 00:42:51,465
I dress carelessly.

661
00:42:54,468 --> 00:42:57,554
Is that the same as me speaking bluntly?

662
00:43:00,849 --> 00:43:02,601
You know you've got a weird talent, right?

663
00:43:03,727 --> 00:43:04,853
You have this ability

664
00:43:05,437 --> 00:43:06,980
to bring people back down to earth.

665
00:43:08,065 --> 00:43:09,566
I'm always floating around

666
00:43:09,650 --> 00:43:12,486
and going all over the place when I talk,

667
00:43:13,070 --> 00:43:14,196
but when I'm with you,

668
00:43:14,697 --> 00:43:16,490
I gracefully…

669
00:43:19,284 --> 00:43:20,619
come back down to earth.

670
00:43:29,253 --> 00:43:30,546
I won't let it happen today, though.

671
00:43:31,964 --> 00:43:33,465
Let's go for round two!

672
00:43:33,549 --> 00:43:36,260
-No! No more soju!
-Let's go!

673
00:43:36,343 --> 00:43:38,387
-Let's go for round two!
-Round two!

674
00:43:38,470 --> 00:43:41,348
I can't… I can't go to karaoke.

675
00:43:41,432 --> 00:43:42,391
Are you going?

676
00:43:42,975 --> 00:43:44,643
No karaoke, right?

677
00:43:44,727 --> 00:43:47,187
-No singing.
-No one's going home tonight!

678
00:43:47,271 --> 00:43:49,398
-She said no one's going home tonight!
-No one!

679
00:43:49,481 --> 00:43:51,024
-We're going to drink more!
-Come on!

680
00:43:51,108 --> 00:43:52,484
Ms. Lee!

681
00:43:52,568 --> 00:43:54,987
Ms. Lee, it's this way!

682
00:43:55,070 --> 00:43:57,448
Oh, I don't drink,

683
00:43:57,531 --> 00:43:59,116
so I'll take my car and meet you there.

684
00:43:59,908 --> 00:44:02,202
Hey, that woman's getting away.

685
00:44:02,786 --> 00:44:03,871
Let's take her car!

686
00:44:03,954 --> 00:44:05,914
-Let's take her car!
-Let's go!

687
00:44:05,998 --> 00:44:07,541
-Let's go!
-Where are we going?

688
00:44:07,624 --> 00:44:08,667
Come on, let's go!

689
00:44:08,751 --> 00:44:09,710
Yeong-su!

690
00:44:11,754 --> 00:44:13,464
Can I come in?

691
00:44:13,547 --> 00:44:15,466
-Can I come in?
-Why, of course!

692
00:44:15,549 --> 00:44:17,593
-Can I come in?
-Please go in!

693
00:44:18,343 --> 00:44:19,678
Come on!

694
00:44:19,762 --> 00:44:22,806
-Let's go!
-Yeong-su's trying to slip out!

695
00:44:22,890 --> 00:44:26,226
-It's 150,000 won for a set menu.
-We're definitely having squid.

696
00:44:26,310 --> 00:44:28,645
Ma'am! Two large squid platters, please!

697
00:44:28,729 --> 00:44:30,230
You can't have squid.

698
00:44:30,314 --> 00:44:32,983
-Just have rockfish.
-But I see them over there!

699
00:44:33,066 --> 00:44:34,151
They're too expensive.

700
00:44:34,234 --> 00:44:36,236
I put a few in there to lure customers.

701
00:44:36,320 --> 00:44:38,280
How expensive can it be? It's just squid!

702
00:44:38,363 --> 00:44:39,782
Two large squid platters!

703
00:44:39,865 --> 00:44:42,201
One squid costs 30,000 won.

704
00:44:42,284 --> 00:44:46,038
Then give us the rockfish, please!

705
00:44:52,795 --> 00:44:56,548
Hey, the customers are leaving.
Let's quiet down!

706
00:44:56,632 --> 00:44:59,092
You quiet down first!

707
00:45:00,677 --> 00:45:02,012
You're so loud.

708
00:45:04,348 --> 00:45:08,769
This is the first time I've ever woken up
excited to go to a meeting.

709
00:45:09,520 --> 00:45:14,399
The thought of meetings used to feel like
a heavy weight on my heart.

710
00:45:16,568 --> 00:45:19,321
I never knew work could be so much fun.

711
00:45:19,404 --> 00:45:20,739
-Where's the squid?
-Don't do that.

712
00:45:23,075 --> 00:45:24,993
Have you had enough to drink?

713
00:45:29,289 --> 00:45:32,459
I like all old movies.

714
00:45:33,752 --> 00:45:35,629
Movies nowadays are the most boring.

715
00:45:36,463 --> 00:45:40,342
With old films,
even the ones labeled as flops

716
00:45:40,425 --> 00:45:42,886
are pretty decent
once you start watching them.

717
00:45:42,970 --> 00:45:44,096
-Right.
-Yeah.

718
00:45:44,179 --> 00:45:46,598
Maybe I'm just nostalgic for the past,

719
00:45:47,808 --> 00:45:50,352
but it makes me feel good
when I see signs for tailor shops

720
00:45:51,186 --> 00:45:53,897
and electronics repair shops in movies.

721
00:45:58,694 --> 00:46:00,195
Do you want to hear an interesting story?

722
00:46:03,490 --> 00:46:04,783
It feels like

723
00:46:05,951 --> 00:46:07,077
we're here in the moment, right?

724
00:46:08,870 --> 00:46:09,913
But actually,

725
00:46:10,831 --> 00:46:12,249
we're just reminiscing.

726
00:46:14,084 --> 00:46:16,503
-Our lives have already ended.
-Okay.

727
00:46:16,587 --> 00:46:18,380
We're 99 years old,

728
00:46:18,463 --> 00:46:19,631
lying in bed,

729
00:46:20,507 --> 00:46:22,009
reliving the times we miss

730
00:46:23,051 --> 00:46:24,803
just before we die.

731
00:46:31,143 --> 00:46:33,103
What we're seeing right now

732
00:46:34,646 --> 00:46:36,064
is a scene from our memories

733
00:46:37,190 --> 00:46:39,192
that we relive just before we die.

734
00:46:53,540 --> 00:46:54,583
I think I get it.

735
00:46:59,129 --> 00:47:01,840
So right now…

736
00:47:04,217 --> 00:47:07,346
is this my memory or yours?

737
00:47:37,209 --> 00:47:40,003
For some reason,
I feel comfortable being with you today.

738
00:47:46,426 --> 00:47:47,302
It always showed

739
00:47:48,053 --> 00:47:50,806
"anxious" or "uneasy" when I was with you.

740
00:47:51,890 --> 00:47:53,850
See this? It's green.

741
00:47:56,353 --> 00:47:59,022
This means you don't hate me.

742
00:47:59,606 --> 00:48:01,775
My emotions are controlled
by the other person.

743
00:48:03,527 --> 00:48:05,737
If they like me, I'm relieved.

744
00:48:06,321 --> 00:48:08,365
If they don't, I'm anxious.

745
00:48:10,075 --> 00:48:13,245
I'm at the mercy
of other people's emotions

746
00:48:13,829 --> 00:48:15,038
because I soak them up like a sponge.

747
00:48:26,842 --> 00:48:28,343
Knock it off.

748
00:48:28,427 --> 00:48:30,887
It's cramped here. Stay still, will you?

749
00:48:32,305 --> 00:48:33,265
Come on, guys.

750
00:48:33,348 --> 00:48:36,184
-Jeez, get off me! What are you doing?
-Seriously?

751
00:48:36,268 --> 00:48:38,645
-For goodness' sake!
-Are you kidding me?

752
00:48:39,646 --> 00:48:41,106
I'm so sick of these two.

753
00:48:51,825 --> 00:48:52,868
This is nice.

754
00:48:57,205 --> 00:48:58,915
This is great!

755
00:49:03,003 --> 00:49:06,590
We have to be
Zorba the Greek at the beach!

756
00:49:06,673 --> 00:49:07,591
It's been forever!

757
00:49:14,806 --> 00:49:18,602
-Dance, dance, dance, yeah.
-Come on, my boy!

758
00:49:21,438 --> 00:49:23,523
To the left first! Here we go!

759
00:49:24,024 --> 00:49:25,734
-One, two!
-One, two!

760
00:49:27,903 --> 00:49:28,945
Step forward!

761
00:49:37,621 --> 00:49:38,538
Again, step forward!

762
00:49:43,293 --> 00:49:44,211
Let us join!

763
00:49:47,130 --> 00:49:48,131
All right!

764
00:49:51,802 --> 00:49:54,095
-All right!
-Let's go!

765
00:50:12,447 --> 00:50:13,657
What a bunch of morons.

766
00:50:30,924 --> 00:50:33,343
Hey, he figured out the ending.

767
00:50:34,469 --> 00:50:36,096
Yeong-su, that's it!

768
00:50:36,847 --> 00:50:38,598
Don't make any more revisions!

769
00:50:47,399 --> 00:50:49,985
I feel like I'll relive this memory
before I die.

770
00:50:53,947 --> 00:50:55,824
Am I really reminiscing right now?

771
00:51:06,459 --> 00:51:07,669
Let's get going!

772
00:51:10,797 --> 00:51:12,299
I can't read your handwriting.

773
00:51:12,382 --> 00:51:15,093
We should watch the sun rise before we go.

774
00:51:15,176 --> 00:51:17,637
After all, we came all the way
to the East Sea.

775
00:51:17,721 --> 00:51:20,473
-Let's watch it before we go!
-Let's do that!

776
00:51:20,557 --> 00:51:22,559
The sun won't rise for another few hours.

777
00:51:22,642 --> 00:51:25,478
We can just grab coffee
at a convenience store and wait.

778
00:51:25,562 --> 00:51:27,397
Just tell me already!

779
00:51:32,152 --> 00:51:33,862
Who likes who exactly?

780
00:51:35,697 --> 00:51:36,948
This energy doesn't come from stamina.

781
00:51:37,699 --> 00:51:39,826
No one our age has this kind of stamina.

782
00:51:40,702 --> 00:51:45,123
Who goes for second and third rounds,
and ends up at a beach at our age?

783
00:51:45,206 --> 00:51:47,042
Clearly, someone likes someone here,

784
00:51:47,125 --> 00:51:48,793
and I'm the sacrificial lamb.

785
00:51:49,669 --> 00:51:50,712
So tell me who it is.

786
00:52:01,181 --> 00:52:02,724
I like Hwang Dong-man.

787
00:52:16,738 --> 00:52:22,202
I like Hwang Dong-man.

788
00:52:22,285 --> 00:52:23,662
Was that a buzz?

789
00:52:23,745 --> 00:52:26,831
And you like me too…

790
00:52:26,915 --> 00:52:28,083
AWKWARD

791
00:52:28,166 --> 00:52:29,000
Huh?

792
00:52:31,419 --> 00:52:32,587
What do you mean "awkward"?

793
00:52:36,383 --> 00:52:37,592
Tell me!

794
00:52:39,844 --> 00:52:40,929
I like…

795
00:52:41,638 --> 00:52:43,431
Ms. Byeon Eun-a.

796
00:52:59,572 --> 00:53:02,117
If I catch any of you texting each other,
you're dead.

797
00:53:05,495 --> 00:53:07,414
And of course, you just stop instantly.

798
00:53:11,501 --> 00:53:13,420
I mean, seriously? "Awkward"?

799
00:53:13,503 --> 00:53:16,339
Why feel awkward?
Did I ask you to like me back?

800
00:53:16,423 --> 00:53:19,426
Who says we can only like one person?
Why are people so narrow-minded?

801
00:53:19,509 --> 00:53:20,677
Byeon Eun-a.

802
00:53:20,760 --> 00:53:21,970
Is that what you think too?

803
00:53:22,053 --> 00:53:25,640
Is it right for him to feel awkward
because I said I like him?

804
00:53:25,724 --> 00:53:28,184
I'd be thrilled if someone I like
said they liked me.

805
00:53:28,268 --> 00:53:29,269
Wouldn't you?

806
00:53:30,937 --> 00:53:32,939
-I would.
-Exactly!

807
00:53:33,648 --> 00:53:35,066
So how could you feel awkward?

808
00:53:36,443 --> 00:53:38,695
Huh? Hey, mister!

809
00:53:38,778 --> 00:53:39,612
Are you sleeping?

810
00:53:40,196 --> 00:53:43,283
-In a situation like this?
-Yeong-su, wake up.

811
00:53:43,366 --> 00:53:44,284
Wake up.

812
00:53:46,411 --> 00:53:48,538
He hasn't slept these past few days.

813
00:53:48,621 --> 00:53:51,666
How could he sleep when I'm so pissed off?

814
00:53:51,750 --> 00:53:53,501
"Awkward"? Are you kidding me?

815
00:53:57,130 --> 00:53:58,798
What the heck is going on?!

816
00:54:00,341 --> 00:54:03,053
Hit the brakes! The brakes!

817
00:54:33,083 --> 00:54:35,502
<i>My wish is to die of old age.</i>

818
00:54:36,294 --> 00:54:37,545
<i>Not from agony,</i>

819
00:54:38,213 --> 00:54:39,589
<i>not from humiliation.</i>

820
00:54:40,715 --> 00:54:41,883
<i>I want to die of old age.</i>

821
00:54:48,515 --> 00:54:49,599
Go sleep inside.

822
00:55:07,367 --> 00:55:08,368
You know…

823
00:55:12,580 --> 00:55:15,166
I can always tell
whether someone's really asleep

824
00:55:16,376 --> 00:55:18,002
or just pretending.

825
00:55:20,255 --> 00:55:22,006
So why are you pretending to sleep?

826
00:55:24,968 --> 00:55:26,010
Just because.

827
00:55:27,804 --> 00:55:29,305
I'm tired.

828
00:56:10,889 --> 00:56:11,931
<i>Step away.</i>

829
00:56:12,724 --> 00:56:15,643
Step away
from Director Lee Jun-hwan's film.

830
00:56:16,269 --> 00:56:18,688
Production costs
will be at least 20 billion won.

831
00:56:19,272 --> 00:56:21,274
What kind of producer debuts
with a 20 billion won film?

832
00:56:21,357 --> 00:56:24,110
Start with something small
and build up your experience.

833
00:56:24,194 --> 00:56:25,737
You can work on a big one next time.

834
00:56:27,113 --> 00:56:29,115
I'm the one who set this in motion,

835
00:56:29,866 --> 00:56:31,951
and I cast Jang Mi-ran.

836
00:56:34,996 --> 00:56:36,247
Didn't you get a paycheck?

837
00:56:36,331 --> 00:56:37,749
Did you work for free?

838
00:56:38,499 --> 00:56:41,294
If you want a stake in this,
work as a freelancer.

839
00:56:41,377 --> 00:56:42,712
You've got some nerve.

840
00:56:42,795 --> 00:56:44,088
You haven't even produced anything,

841
00:56:44,172 --> 00:56:46,007
and you think you can handle
a 20-billion-won project?

842
00:56:46,090 --> 00:56:47,592
Do you know what investing is?

843
00:56:47,675 --> 00:56:49,010
Have you ever worked with a VC?

844
00:56:49,093 --> 00:56:51,971
Do you know anything about camera models,
budgets, or how many you need?

845
00:56:52,555 --> 00:56:53,640
Of course, you don't.

846
00:56:59,062 --> 00:57:00,605
Was it Ms. Oh Jeong-hui's request

847
00:57:02,565 --> 00:57:03,608
to kick me off the project?

848
00:57:08,488 --> 00:57:10,448
What do you take me for, a lackey?

849
00:57:10,531 --> 00:57:13,534
Do I look like someone who takes orders
and acts on them?

850
00:57:13,618 --> 00:57:17,121
Choi Film is my company,
and it's my money!

851
00:57:34,847 --> 00:57:36,558
<i>Ms. Oh Jeong-hui would like to see you.</i>

852
00:57:37,225 --> 00:57:39,686
<i>I'll be waiting in the car near Choi Film.</i>

853
00:58:25,064 --> 00:58:27,358
You told me not to let anyone find out,

854
00:58:28,735 --> 00:58:31,195
so how does it make any sense
that you're getting close to Mi-ran?

855
00:58:32,572 --> 00:58:35,325
If you decided to change your name
and live as a different person,

856
00:58:35,408 --> 00:58:37,243
you never should've
stepped into this world.

857
00:58:37,994 --> 00:58:39,120
It was Dad's world too.

858
00:58:39,746 --> 00:58:41,706
A place he walked away from
without any respect.

859
00:58:42,790 --> 00:58:45,209
I wondered what the big deal was
with the people here.

860
00:58:46,586 --> 00:58:47,795
Turns out, they're nothing special.

861
00:58:48,671 --> 00:58:50,673
They have nothing but elitist attitudes

862
00:58:50,757 --> 00:58:51,924
and are painfully old-fashioned.

863
00:58:58,014 --> 00:58:59,599
Is that why you're seeing a guy like that?

864
00:59:00,642 --> 00:59:02,268
Out of pity for your dad?

865
00:59:03,770 --> 00:59:05,396
I know why you find him appealing.

866
00:59:05,980 --> 00:59:07,190
He's wild and unruly,

867
00:59:08,566 --> 00:59:10,526
like a half-grown boy.

868
00:59:11,110 --> 00:59:12,695
But he has potential, right?

869
00:59:13,404 --> 00:59:14,989
See for yourself if he really does.

870
00:59:15,615 --> 00:59:18,159
The most dreadful thing in the world
is an incompetent man.

871
00:59:18,826 --> 00:59:21,579
An incompetent man
is nothing more than a castrated male.

872
00:59:25,750 --> 00:59:26,584
So tell me.

873
00:59:28,127 --> 00:59:30,505
Are you satisfied
with your competent husband?

874
00:59:32,757 --> 00:59:34,300
When I look at the streets,

875
00:59:35,843 --> 00:59:37,970
I don't see people walking.

876
00:59:38,054 --> 00:59:40,765
I see masses of emotion walking around.

877
00:59:41,349 --> 00:59:43,643
You can see their complete emotional state

878
00:59:44,268 --> 00:59:46,646
in every step they take
and every expression they make.

879
00:59:48,815 --> 00:59:50,650
That's why I don't see them as people

880
00:59:51,651 --> 00:59:53,194
but as masses of emotion.

881
00:59:54,904 --> 00:59:57,281
Do you know what mass of emotion you are?

882
01:00:00,201 --> 01:00:01,327
A mass of contempt.

883
01:00:03,371 --> 01:00:06,666
Have you ever looked at someone
sitting across from you without contempt?

884
01:00:08,626 --> 01:00:10,044
You find everyone pathetic,

885
01:00:11,003 --> 01:00:12,422
and you don't like anyone.

886
01:00:13,423 --> 01:00:15,883
After leaving
your pathetic husband and child,

887
01:00:16,467 --> 01:00:20,096
you replaced them
with a shiny new husband and child.

888
01:00:22,682 --> 01:00:24,934
So isn't it about time
you let go of that contempt?

889
01:00:26,811 --> 01:00:29,021
But I guess something inside you
is still missing.

890
01:00:49,208 --> 01:00:51,419
NO ONE COMES INTO YOUR LIFE BY MISTAKE

891
01:00:53,254 --> 01:00:57,091
<i>I contacted an agency to see</i>
<i>if they could look up Byeon Si-on online.</i>

892
01:00:57,675 --> 01:00:59,469
They found a file from your high school

893
01:01:00,303 --> 01:01:02,930
that listed your guardian as "Ga Su-ja."

894
01:01:03,639 --> 01:01:04,891
EMERGENCY CONTACT LIST

895
01:01:04,974 --> 01:01:07,310
NAME: BYEON SI-ON
GUARDIAN: GA SU-JA

896
01:01:07,393 --> 01:01:09,812
-Here you go.
-Thank you.

897
01:01:09,896 --> 01:01:11,939
Thank you. Have a good day.

898
01:01:17,737 --> 01:01:19,947
<i>It's a unique name, so it'll be easy</i>
<i>for people to track her down.</i>

899
01:01:20,573 --> 01:01:22,366
The file has been deleted,

900
01:01:22,450 --> 01:01:23,910
but someone might've seen it already,

901
01:01:24,410 --> 01:01:26,329
so you should move out of that house.

902
01:01:31,751 --> 01:01:33,586
This is a brand-new
studio apartment in Gangbuk.

903
01:01:33,669 --> 01:01:36,339
It's fully furnished,
so you just have to move in.

904
01:01:40,009 --> 01:01:40,927
And this is a cash card.

905
01:01:41,427 --> 01:01:44,096
The PIN is the last four digits
of your phone number.

906
01:01:59,195 --> 01:02:01,280
Let me know
if you don't like living there.

907
01:02:01,364 --> 01:02:02,448
I'll find another place.

908
01:02:03,366 --> 01:02:04,617
I'll even buy you one if you want.

909
01:02:05,743 --> 01:02:08,496
You said you wouldn't let anyone find out

910
01:02:09,163 --> 01:02:10,873
that you're actually the poor,
little Byeon Si-on.

911
01:02:11,499 --> 01:02:12,375
So just move out.

912
01:02:12,959 --> 01:02:14,126
You're embarrassed

913
01:02:15,419 --> 01:02:16,504
that I live in a place like that

914
01:02:17,255 --> 01:02:18,297
with my step-grandmother.

915
01:02:18,381 --> 01:02:20,216
Yeah, it's embarrassing.

916
01:02:21,175 --> 01:02:23,845
It's embarrassing,
and I'm bound to get criticized.

917
01:02:24,929 --> 01:02:27,765
No one would care
if you lived with your dad there.

918
01:02:27,849 --> 01:02:30,059
But it's a different situation
with him gone.

919
01:02:39,944 --> 01:02:42,113
I wish I had been born
from a bamboo shoot.

920
01:02:43,030 --> 01:02:44,198
It would've been so nice

921
01:02:44,699 --> 01:02:46,367
to have been born out of thin air,

922
01:02:47,660 --> 01:02:49,745
with no need for sperm, eggs,
a mother, or a father.

923
01:02:56,294 --> 01:02:57,503
<i>Just know this.</i>

924
01:02:59,255 --> 01:03:01,549
<i>It's not your fault</i>
<i>I didn't turn out better.</i>

925
01:03:03,175 --> 01:03:05,219
But I'm going to be really successful.

926
01:03:06,929 --> 01:03:08,931
But when I imagine myself doing well,

927
01:03:10,182 --> 01:03:13,019
I get pissed off
thinking about you showing up…

928
01:03:15,396 --> 01:03:16,522
smiling and clinging to me.

929
01:03:18,983 --> 01:03:21,402
A mother rejecting a child
for not being good enough is one thing.

930
01:03:22,653 --> 01:03:25,531
But a mother who clings
to a successful one like a leech…

931
01:03:27,575 --> 01:03:28,701
is even worse.

932
01:03:33,164 --> 01:03:34,165
So don't ever

933
01:03:35,833 --> 01:03:36,834
act like…

934
01:03:39,086 --> 01:03:40,296
we're close.

935
01:04:34,016 --> 01:04:35,017
<i>Help me.</i>

936
01:04:38,104 --> 01:04:38,938
MS. BYEON EUN-A

937
01:04:53,869 --> 01:04:55,788
<i>The person you are trying to reach</i>
<i>is unavailable…</i>

938
01:05:55,723 --> 01:05:57,516
WE ARE ALL TRYING HERE

939
01:06:15,618 --> 01:06:16,994
This screenplay is insane.

940
01:06:17,078 --> 01:06:18,329
<i>Keep a room open tonight.</i>

941
01:06:18,412 --> 01:06:20,372
<i>Noh Kang-sik will be coming.</i>

942
01:06:20,456 --> 01:06:23,042
<i>Make sure Dong-man stays away</i>
<i>from Agit today.</i>

943
01:06:23,125 --> 01:06:25,461
<i>Why are you refusing the money</i>
<i>and the house?</i>

944
01:06:25,544 --> 01:06:28,631
Would it be okay if I registered
my grandma's address at your place?

945
01:06:28,714 --> 01:06:30,633
-Who's the director again?
-Nice to meet you.

946
01:06:30,716 --> 01:06:33,594
<i>When someone does well,</i>
<i>I really feel sick to my stomach.</i>

947
01:06:33,677 --> 01:06:36,263
<i>Don't ruin something</i>
<i>someone poured everything into.</i>

948
01:06:36,347 --> 01:06:37,306
<i>Let's produce this ourselves.</i>

949
01:06:37,389 --> 01:06:38,224
We'll take it from here.

950
01:06:38,307 --> 01:06:39,600
The director said he'd work with me.

951
01:06:39,683 --> 01:06:41,060
I did it because nobody else can!

952
01:06:41,143 --> 01:06:44,355
I did it because everyone's afraid
of a piece of trash like him!

953
01:06:45,356 --> 01:06:50,027
Subtitle translation by: Jenny Kim

